1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,294
In class, names are called by
roll number, not by height.

2
00:00:04,294 --> 00:00:07,702
Houses on a street go by
door number, not by size.

3
00:00:07,702 --> 00:00:10,993
In an exam hall, seats
follow the register number.

4
00:00:11,393 --> 00:00:14,135
Each list works because
it uses the right number.

5
00:00:14,135 --> 00:00:17,013
Pick the wrong one, and
things fall out of order.

6
00:00:17,463 --> 00:00:20,125
Diya spots this on the
college notice board.

7
00:00:20,125 --> 00:00:23,032
One name on the seating
list sits out of place.

8
00:00:23,382 --> 00:00:25,727
Mendeleev's table had the same problem.

9
00:00:25,727 --> 00:00:28,427
A few elements sat out of order by weight.

10
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:33,011
By the end of this video, you will
know the number that fixed it, and how

11
00:00:33,011 --> 00:00:35,263
to name an element nobody has made yet.

12
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:37,602
So what is that number?

13
00:00:37,602 --> 00:00:39,083
Look inside an atom.

14
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:41,661
At the centre is the nucleus.

15
00:00:41,661 --> 00:00:46,041
It holds protons, and each proton
carries one positive charge.

16
00:00:46,391 --> 00:00:50,034
The number of protons is the
atomic number, written Z.

17
00:00:50,034 --> 00:00:52,969
Hydrogen has one proton, so its Z is one.

18
00:00:52,969 --> 00:00:54,147
Carbon has six.

19
00:00:54,547 --> 00:00:58,223
In a neutral atom, the
electrons equal the protons.

20
00:00:58,223 --> 00:01:00,532
So Z also counts the electrons.

21
00:01:00,932 --> 00:01:04,151
Change Z by one, and you
get a different element.

22
00:01:04,151 --> 00:01:07,911
Mass can change, but Z is the
identity card of an element.

23
00:01:08,561 --> 00:01:13,135
In nineteen thirteen, the English
physicist Henry Moseley showed this number

24
00:01:13,135 --> 00:01:14,415
is the true key.

25
00:01:14,415 --> 00:01:17,272
His result gave us the
modern periodic law.

26
00:01:17,922 --> 00:01:20,802
Let's run his test on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:20,802 --> 00:01:24,009
A beam of fast electrons
hits a metal target.

28
00:01:24,359 --> 00:01:26,445
The metal gives out X rays.

29
00:01:26,445 --> 00:01:29,511
Each element gives its
own X ray frequency.

30
00:01:29,911 --> 00:01:32,738
Moseley did this for
one metal after another.

31
00:01:32,738 --> 00:01:36,750
Calcium, titanium, iron, cobalt,
nickel, copper, and more.

32
00:01:37,150 --> 00:01:41,080
He plotted the square root of the
frequency against atomic number.

33
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:43,433
The points fall on one straight line.

34
00:01:43,833 --> 00:01:46,759
Now plot the same points
against atomic mass.

35
00:01:46,759 --> 00:01:48,419
Watch cobalt and nickel.

36
00:01:48,419 --> 00:01:51,444
Nickel is lighter, yet
its point lies higher.

37
00:01:51,844 --> 00:01:53,998
Against mass, the line breaks.

38
00:01:53,998 --> 00:01:56,563
Against atomic number, it is perfect.

39
00:01:56,563 --> 00:01:59,759
So atomic number is more
basic than atomic mass.

40
00:02:00,409 --> 00:02:02,831
That gives the modern periodic law.

41
00:02:02,831 --> 00:02:07,408
The physical and chemical properties
of the elements are periodic functions

42
00:02:07,408 --> 00:02:09,142
of their atomic numbers.

43
00:02:09,542 --> 00:02:10,973
Why does it repeat?

44
00:02:10,973 --> 00:02:15,514
Because the arrangement of the outer
electrons repeats, and Z decides that

45
00:02:15,514 --> 00:02:16,419
arrangement.

46
00:02:17,069 --> 00:02:18,852
Here is the side by side.

47
00:02:18,852 --> 00:02:21,661
Order by mass, and cobalt and nickel swap.

48
00:02:21,661 --> 00:02:24,719
Order by atomic number,
and they fall in line.

49
00:02:25,119 --> 00:02:26,258
Pause and think.

50
00:02:26,258 --> 00:02:28,157
Nickel is lighter than cobalt.

51
00:02:28,157 --> 00:02:30,675
In the modern table, which comes first?

52
00:02:33,875 --> 00:02:34,775
Cobalt!

53
00:02:34,555 --> 00:02:37,830
Cobalt is twenty seven,
nickel is twenty eight.

54
00:02:37,830 --> 00:02:40,785
Atomic number decides the order, not mass.

55
00:02:41,435 --> 00:02:42,482
Remember this.

56
00:02:42,482 --> 00:02:45,800
Atomic number, not atomic
mass, is the true key.

57
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:48,235
Now, one worked example.

58
00:02:48,535 --> 00:02:53,319
Elements above one hundred get a
temporary name from IUPAC, the world body

59
00:02:53,319 --> 00:02:54,797
for chemistry names.

60
00:02:54,797 --> 00:02:57,721
It stays until an official name is agreed.

61
00:02:58,221 --> 00:02:59,822
Each digit has a root.

62
00:02:59,822 --> 00:03:05,014
Nil for zero, un for one, bi for
two, tri, quad, pent, hex, sept, oct,

63
00:03:05,014 --> 00:03:06,314
and enn for nine.

64
00:03:06,814 --> 00:03:09,574
Let's name element one
hundred and nineteen.

65
00:03:10,074 --> 00:03:10,974
Step one.

66
00:03:10,892 --> 00:03:12,887
Split the number into digits.

67
00:03:12,887 --> 00:03:14,263
One, one, nine.

68
00:03:15,013 --> 00:03:15,913
Step two.

69
00:03:15,853 --> 00:03:17,964
Swap each digit for its root.

70
00:03:17,964 --> 00:03:19,224
Un, un, enn.

71
00:03:19,974 --> 00:03:21,047
Step three.

72
00:03:21,047 --> 00:03:23,227
Join them and add i u m.

73
00:03:23,227 --> 00:03:24,229
Ununennium.

74
00:03:24,979 --> 00:03:25,879
Step four.

75
00:03:25,875 --> 00:03:29,109
The symbol takes the
first letter of each root.

76
00:03:29,109 --> 00:03:30,170
U, u, e.

77
00:03:30,170 --> 00:03:32,045
So the symbol is U u e.

78
00:03:32,795 --> 00:03:34,260
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:34,610 --> 00:03:39,333
Many students put potassium before
argon, because potassium is lighter.

80
00:03:39,733 --> 00:03:44,361
But argon's atomic number is
eighteen, and potassium's is nineteen.

81
00:03:44,361 --> 00:03:45,826
Argon comes first.

82
00:03:45,826 --> 00:03:48,085
Always order by atomic number.

83
00:03:48,735 --> 00:03:50,263
Where is this asked?

84
00:03:50,263 --> 00:03:54,972
As one mark questions on the modern
periodic law, and on IUPAC names and

85
00:03:54,972 --> 00:03:57,546
symbols of elements above one hundred.

86
00:03:58,146 --> 00:03:59,373
Exam level now.

87
00:03:59,373 --> 00:04:04,029
What is the IUPAC temporary symbol
for the element with atomic number one

88
00:04:04,029 --> 00:04:05,619
hundred and seventeen?

89
00:04:05,619 --> 00:04:06,898
Pause and solve.

90
00:04:12,298 --> 00:04:13,952
The answer is A.

91
00:04:13,952 --> 00:04:14,887
U u s.

92
00:04:15,387 --> 00:04:18,074
Split one hundred seventeen into digits.

93
00:04:18,074 --> 00:04:19,547
One, one, seven.

94
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:22,676
The roots are un, un, and sept.

95
00:04:22,676 --> 00:04:24,708
Join them and add i u m.

96
00:04:24,708 --> 00:04:25,697
Ununseptium.

97
00:04:26,197 --> 00:04:28,094
First letters give U u s.

98
00:04:28,094 --> 00:04:30,563
B is element one hundred and seven.

99
00:04:30,563 --> 00:04:32,665
C is one hundred and sixteen.

100
00:04:33,265 --> 00:04:34,725
Here is your recap.

101
00:04:34,725 --> 00:04:36,227
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:39,427 --> 00:04:42,425
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

103
00:04:42,825 --> 00:04:43,725
One.

104
00:04:43,330 --> 00:04:46,489
What does the atomic
number of an element count?

105
00:04:47,289 --> 00:04:48,189
Two.

106
00:04:47,823 --> 00:04:52,390
Why does argon come before potassium,
even though argon is heavier?

107
00:04:53,190 --> 00:04:54,090
Three.

108
00:04:53,810 --> 00:04:58,203
What is the IUPAC temporary name
of element one hundred and twenty?

109
00:04:59,103 --> 00:05:03,057
Next time: why does the table
have exactly eighteen columns?

110
00:05:03,057 --> 00:05:06,871
We find out in the structure
of the modern periodic table.
